Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.72,0:00:01.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Hello Everybody
Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.94,0:00:04.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Today we will be talking about our work
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.06,0:00:10.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}CubeSat Magnetic Atlas and in-Orbit Compensation of Residual Magnetic Dipole
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.34,0:00:14.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Magnetic disturbances from satellite subsystems
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.40,0:00:18.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} have concerned many satellite missions over the lifetime of space industry
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.40,0:00:21.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Whether for purposes of study of surrounding field data
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.84,0:00:25.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}like in old satellites missions CASSINI and THEMIS
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.30,0:00:27.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}or for purposes of magnetic attitude control
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.96,0:00:30.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Electromagnetic cleanness program (EMC) is a technique
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.92,0:00:33.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}is a technique that was developed to handle this problem
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.84,0:00:36.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} through defining the magnetic sources and then either
Dialogue: 0,0:00:36.42,0:00:40.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}shield them, compensate them or eliminate them to a specified limit
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.76,0:00:44.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The identification is done through measurements in huge facilities
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.28,0:00:46.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}with external field cancellation
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.56,0:00:50.44,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}For a LEO cubesat that plans to use magnetic control
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.44,0:00:52.22,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}like PRISM cubesat
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.22,0:00:54.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}this procedure isn`t enough
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.02,0:00:56.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} and it still suffers from residual magnetization
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.82,0:00:58.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}that disturbs its magnetic control
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.78,0:01:03.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which requires further sources identification and elementation
Dialogue: 0,0:01:03.76,0:01:05.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Through this work
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.04,0:01:08.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}we are proposing a complementing procedure to EMC
Dialogue: 0,0:01:08.28,0:01:11.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} in which all residual magnetic disturbances are modeled
Dialogue: 0,0:01:11.54,0:01:15.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and fed to extended kalman filter to produce an atlas
Dialogue: 0,0:01:15.34,0:01:18.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}that could be used in the cubesat control
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.08,0:01:20.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}to modify its performance
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.60,0:01:23.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}There are two components to consider
Dialogue: 0,0:01:23.42,0:01:25.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}in magnetic attitude and control
Dialogue: 0,0:01:25.62,0:01:28.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}earth's magnetic field measurement through magnetometer
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.88,0:01:31.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and control through magnetorquers
Dialogue: 0,0:01:31.30,0:01:33.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Disturbances affect both sides
Dialogue: 0,0:01:33.30,0:01:37.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} in the first step as residual magnetic field of the satellite is measured
Dialogue: 0,0:01:37.58,0:01:41.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and  in the second step as addition of a magnetization value
Dialogue: 0,0:01:42.34,0:01:44.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The sources for this are many
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.14,0:01:47.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and we intend to handle only wire tracks in this work
Dialogue: 0,0:01:48.88,0:01:51.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The plan is to start with satellite design
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.00,0:01:53.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}import it to our software tool
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.84,0:01:55.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which should produce a magnetic field
Dialogue: 0,0:01:55.82,0:01:58.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and torque distribution in the satellites size
Dialogue: 0,0:01:58.86,0:02:01.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}This tool would need verification and correction
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.34,0:02:03.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which we plan to use hardware measurements for
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.58,0:02:07.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} The final correct model should then be imported
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.10,0:02:10.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}to the satellite control simulation to test them
Dialogue: 0,0:02:10.06,0:02:11.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and establish satellite control
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.76,0:02:13.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and maximum values allowed
Dialogue: 0,0:02:13.46,0:02:14.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}in the field distribution
Dialogue: 0,0:02:15.62,0:02:17.32,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}These values could now be used to
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.38,0:02:18.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}optimize the satellite design
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.68,0:02:22.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Finally, the control would be implemented on the satellite
Dialogue: 0,0:02:24.56,0:02:26.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Our work is in wires
Dialogue: 0,0:02:26.58,0:02:28.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Biot and savat law for magnetic field
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.54,0:02:31.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}due to current in wire is the following one you see in the slide
Dialogue: 0,0:02:32.16,0:02:35.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}with r as the distance from the point of measurement to the wire
Dialogue: 0,0:02:36.40,0:02:38.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}We can deduce the force and torque on a wire
Dialogue: 0,0:02:38.38,0:02:41.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}due to an external magnetic field in the following equations
Dialogue: 0,0:02:42.34,0:02:45.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}We are however more interested in wires participation
Dialogue: 0,0:02:45.16,0:02:47.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}rather than the external field
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.48,0:02:51.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}so some further adjustments lead us to the M capital matrix
Dialogue: 0,0:02:51.48,0:02:53.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which is an expression of wire magnetization
Dialogue: 0,0:02:53.90,0:02:55.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}so we are also calculating it
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.78,0:03:00.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The magnetorquers use a vector form for magnetic moment however
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.94,0:03:02.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Which is m_ctrl here
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.08,0:03:05.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}so later some adjustments will be done on the matrix
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.62,0:03:07.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}to be possible to use it
Dialogue: 0,0:03:08.78,0:03:10.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}For the purposes of this work
Dialogue: 0,0:03:10.82,0:03:13.32,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}we were limited to analysis of some parts of
Dialogue: 0,0:03:13.32,0:03:15.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} the energy power system of the satellite
Dialogue: 0,0:03:15.76,0:03:18.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which are solar panels and EPS PCB
Dialogue: 0,0:03:18.24,0:03:20.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}along with on board computer PCB
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.34,0:03:24.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The components are placed in their position
Dialogue: 0,0:03:24.08,0:03:26.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} in the real satellite as seen in the image
Dialogue: 0,0:03:28.62,0:03:31.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}PCBs were available in the design software
Dialogue: 0,0:03:31.88,0:03:34.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and we had then to import them to be read
Dialogue: 0,0:03:34.06,0:03:36.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}as tracks in MATLAB without components
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.42,0:03:40.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Current direction and values were required for the solution
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.86,0:03:43.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}so some assumptions were made to produce them
Dialogue: 0,0:03:43.52,0:03:44.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Which you can see in the slide
Dialogue: 0,0:03:45.42,0:03:49.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}We got to detect and analyze all the tracks in red
Dialogue: 0,0:03:50.02,0:03:53.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and Solar panels as you have seen before were modeled
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.52,0:03:55.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}as wires which are the green ones
Dialogue: 0,0:03:55.96,0:03:57.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and were double wired in a technique to
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.92,0:03:59.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}remove their magnetization effect
Dialogue: 0,0:04:01.40,0:04:04.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The results for magnetic field models are in two forms
Dialogue: 0,0:04:05.40,0:04:08.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}constant due to PCBs like in the figure here
Dialogue: 0,0:04:09.82,0:04:12.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and time varying due to the change in
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.24,0:04:14.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}solar panels exposure to the sun
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.68,0:04:16.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Like seen in the GIF in the top
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.80,0:04:21.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Now that the magnetic disturbance models are set
Dialogue: 0,0:04:21.94,0:04:25.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}we shall proceed to describe the algorithms for mapping
Dialogue: 0,0:04:25.24,0:04:28.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}identifying and rejecting the magnetic disturbances
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.74,0:04:31.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}We shall consider an example mission of
Dialogue: 0,0:04:31.26,0:04:34.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} a magnetically controlled 3U CubeSat in the ISS orbit
Dialogue: 0,0:04:35.22,0:04:38.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The attitude control is to align the body frame and the orbital frame
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.28,0:04:41.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}We will show the performance of 
Dialogue: 0,0:04:41.14,0:04:44.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}a magnetometer-based Extended Kalman filter to estimate
Dialogue: 0,0:04:44.52,0:04:47.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} both the state variables and the residual magnetization
Dialogue: 0,0:04:48.52,0:04:50.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The filter maps the magnetic disturbances
Dialogue: 0,0:04:50.78,0:04:54.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} to the residual magnetic moment and the magnetometer bias
Dialogue: 0,0:04:54.76,0:04:57.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}thus creating a set of maps 
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.44,0:04:59.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}for all principal regimes of the spacecraft
Dialogue: 0,0:05:01.80,0:05:06.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}We use quaternion kinematics and Euler dynamical equations
Dialogue: 0,0:05:06.28,0:05:08.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}to model the rotations of the spacecraft
Dialogue: 0,0:05:08.82,0:05:11.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The model includes the gravity torque 
Dialogue: 0,0:05:11.18,0:05:13.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and the control torque produced by the magnetorquers
Dialogue: 0,0:05:14.24,0:05:17.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Other environmental torques are evaluated to be
Dialogue: 0,0:05:17.44,0:05:20.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}one order of magnitude smaller than the magnetic disturbance
Dialogue: 0,0:05:20.84,0:05:23.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}hence they are modeled as random noise
Dialogue: 0,0:05:24.72,0:05:28.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}It is important to note here the torque due to
Dialogue: 0,0:05:28.46,0:05:30.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} residual magnetization that comes directly
Dialogue: 0,0:05:30.78,0:05:33.44,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}from the matrices described earlier
Dialogue: 0,0:05:34.64,0:05:37.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}A Lyapunov-based magnetic PD controller
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.72,0:05:39.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}is used in the attitude control loop
Dialogue: 0,0:05:40.36,0:05:41.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The control loop parameters are
Dialogue: 0,0:05:41.96,0:05:43.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} 5 seconds for control 
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.28,0:05:45.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and 1 second for measurements
Dialogue: 0,0:05:45.50,0:05:47.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The plot shows the required control moment
Dialogue: 0,0:05:47.94,0:05:51.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which is of the order of ten to the power of -3
Dialogue: 0,0:05:52.30,0:05:56.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}This sets a limit to the admissible level of magnetization
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.06,0:06:00.38,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} If we assume a 10% error in identification
Dialogue: 0,0:06:00.78,0:06:03.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}of the magnetic disturbance by EKF then
Dialogue: 0,0:06:04.76,0:06:08.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}ten to the power of -3 magnetic moment distorts
Dialogue: 0,0:06:08.40,0:06:11.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}the control action by 10%
Dialogue: 0,0:06:11.16,0:06:13.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which is enough to degrade the control quality
Dialogue: 0,0:06:14.48,0:06:18.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}If magnetic disturbances are not identified and rejected
Dialogue: 0,0:06:18.92,0:06:22.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}he spacecraft is rendered uncontrollable
Dialogue: 0,0:06:22.84,0:06:26.38,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} when the value of residual magnetic moment 
Dialogue: 0,0:06:26.38,0:06:29.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}is already 1e-4 which is really small
Dialogue: 0,0:06:31.08,0:06:34.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}We introduce the EKF that identifies 
Dialogue: 0,0:06:34.72,0:06:36.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}the attitude quaternion
Dialogue: 0,0:06:36.10,0:06:38.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}the vector of angular velocity
Dialogue: 0,0:06:38.16,0:06:40.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} and the residual magnetic moment
Dialogue: 0,0:06:40.16,0:06:42.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} and the magnetometer bias
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.86,0:06:45.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}This slide presents simulation results  for our 
Dialogue: 0,0:06:45.28,0:06:48.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}model with a constant residual magnetic moment
Dialogue: 0,0:06:49.26,0:06:52.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The plot shows how the control quality
Dialogue: 0,0:06:52.12,0:06:54.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}degrades as the magnitude of
Dialogue: 0,0:06:54.82,0:06:56.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}the residual magnetic moment increases
Dialogue: 0,0:06:57.46,0:07:00.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}It is seen that the adcs performance
Dialogue: 0,0:07:00.78,0:07:03.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}expressed through Euler angles
Dialogue: 0,0:07:03.38,0:07:05.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}is still acceptable when the magnitude 
Dialogue: 0,0:07:05.62,0:07:12.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}of residual moment is at 2.5*ten to the power of -3
Dialogue: 0,0:07:12.16,0:07:15.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which agrees with the earlier estimates
Dialogue: 0,0:07:16.64,0:07:18.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}This is one of the examples of
Dialogue: 0,0:07:18.68,0:07:21.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}the system’s controlled dynamics in
Dialogue: 0,0:07:21.00,0:07:23.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}the presence of magnetic disturbances
Dialogue: 0,0:07:23.16,0:07:25.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which are identified and rejected
Dialogue: 0,0:07:26.12,0:07:29.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The relative error of magnetic moment identification
Dialogue: 0,0:07:29.90,0:07:34.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} is shown as bounded by 4:6% in each component
Dialogue: 0,0:07:36.54,0:07:38.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Now we shall adopt the matrices
Dialogue: 0,0:07:39.46,0:07:41.38,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}obtained from the magnetization model
Dialogue: 0,0:07:41.38,0:07:43.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} to simulate the spacecraft dynamics
Dialogue: 0,0:07:43.16,0:07:46.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} with varying magnetic disturbances
Dialogue: 0,0:07:47.26,0:07:49.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The torque and bias matrices
Dialogue: 0,0:07:49.62,0:07:51.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}are rescaled for this example
Dialogue: 0,0:07:51.14,0:07:54.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}to produce an adequate level of disturbance
Dialogue: 0,0:07:54.42,0:07:57.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}It is also shown here that the magnetization
Dialogue: 0,0:07:57.20,0:07:59.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} due to solar panels is changing
Dialogue: 0,0:07:59.10,0:08:01.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}depending on the spacecraft attitude
Dialogue: 0,0:08:01.46,0:08:04.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and the incident angle of sunlight
Dialogue: 0,0:08:05.42,0:08:07.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}This is an example with
Dialogue: 0,0:08:07.36,0:08:09.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}the varying magnetic disturbance
Dialogue: 0,0:08:10.60,0:08:13.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The disturbance values are shown above the plot
Dialogue: 0,0:08:13.66,0:08:16.22,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Resulting pointing accuracy is about 5 degrees
Dialogue: 0,0:08:16.60,0:08:18.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} The result is not different from 
Dialogue: 0,0:08:18.78,0:08:22.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}our simulations with the constant
Dialogue: 0,0:08:22.40,0:08:24.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} residual magnetic moment
Dialogue: 0,0:08:24.16,0:08:25.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which means that the filter can
Dialogue: 0,0:08:25.74,0:08:27.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}track the varying disturbance
Dialogue: 0,0:08:29.18,0:08:32.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}With a sudden change of regime
Dialogue: 0,0:08:32.68,0:08:34.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}as shown in the plots here
Dialogue: 0,0:08:35.30,0:08:37.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}magnetic disturbance can also change
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.92,0:08:41.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} In this example the control quality becomes worse
Dialogue: 0,0:08:41.50,0:08:44.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} and it takes about two hours to recover
Dialogue: 0,0:08:44.56,0:08:47.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The filter converged to certain estimates
Dialogue: 0,0:08:47.70,0:08:50.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}of magnetic moment and magnetometer bias
Dialogue: 0,0:08:51.10,0:08:53.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}These values are taken as
Dialogue: 0,0:08:53.02,0:08:54.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} the initial guess for this regime
Dialogue: 0,0:08:54.98,0:08:58.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} Obtaining these values for all principal regimes
Dialogue: 0,0:08:58.44,0:09:01.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} is equivalent to having an atlas of magnetic disturbances
Dialogue: 0,0:09:02.86,0:09:05.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}This slide shows what happens if we feed
Dialogue: 0,0:09:05.62,0:09:08.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}the magnetic moment and magnetometer bias estimated
Dialogue: 0,0:09:09.38,0:09:14.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} for the regime to the filter exactly when the regime starts
Dialogue: 0,0:09:15.90,0:09:18.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}This research has been important for us
Dialogue: 0,0:09:18.08,0:09:20.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}because the work we do is going to
Dialogue: 0,0:09:20.10,0:09:22.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}be a part of the Skoltech Swarm mission
Dialogue: 0,0:09:22.68,0:09:25.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}comprising 4 magnetically controlled CubeSats
Dialogue: 0,0:09:26.34,0:09:28.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}So far the learning outcomes are
Dialogue: 0,0:09:29.10,0:09:31.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}The magnetic field produced through PCBs
Dialogue: 0,0:09:31.36,0:09:32.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}poses a significant value 
Dialogue: 0,0:09:32.98,0:09:34.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} that it should be monitored
Dialogue: 0,0:09:34.30,0:09:37.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}over the operation regimes of the satellite
Dialogue: 0,0:09:37.82,0:09:40.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Having an atlas of magnetic disturbances
Dialogue: 0,0:09:40.16,0:09:42.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}does enhance EKF performance
Dialogue: 0,0:09:42.74,0:09:44.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}which gives our mission more time
Dialogue: 0,0:09:44.46,0:09:46.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}to spend for its scientific tasks 
Dialogue: 0,0:09:47.38,0:09:49.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}We should the operational example
Dialogue: 0,0:09:49.26,0:09:52.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}of the magnetometer-based EKF
Dialogue: 0,0:09:52.64,0:09:55.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1} but we are going to fuse additional sensors
Dialogue: 0,0:09:55.56,0:09:58.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}gyros and sun sensors in our mission
Dialogue: 0,0:09:58.42,0:10:01.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}and this will make the proposed EKF
Dialogue: 0,0:10:01.02,0:10:02.48,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}even more robust 
Dialogue: 0,0:10:02.48,0:10:04.48,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fs72\b1\fnArial Narrow\i1}Thank you for your attention
Dialogue: 0,0:10:04.48,0:10:06.48,Default,,0,0,0,,
